---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GcdLcmCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/GcdLcmCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="gcd-lcm-calculator">
  <GcdLcmCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>
      Type two or more whole numbers separated by commas, spaces or newlines. The GCD and LCM calculator
      shows the greatest common divisor and least common multiple as you type. It also shows the Euclidean
      algorithm table for the first pair, the prime factorisation of each input, and the steps for both
      answers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Negative signs are ignored. GCD and LCM are defined on magnitudes, so GCD(−12, 18) is the same as
      GCD(12, 18).
    </p>

    <h2>What the two quantities mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The greatest common divisor (GCD) is the largest whole number that divides all of your inputs
        exactly. It is also called the greatest common factor (GCF) or highest common factor (HCF). The three
        names mean the same thing.
      </li>
      <li>
        The least common multiple (LCM) is the smallest positive number that all of your inputs divide into
        exactly.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If the GCD is 1, the numbers are coprime: they share no prime factor, but neither has to be prime
      itself. 8 and 9 are coprime.
    </p>

    <h2>The Euclidean algorithm</h2>
    <p>
      This is the oldest algorithm still in everyday use. Divide the larger number by the smaller, keep the
      remainder, and repeat with the smaller number and that remainder. When the remainder is zero, the last
      non-zero divisor is the GCD.
    </p>
    <p>For 1071 and 462:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">a</th><th scope="col">b</th><th scope="col">a ÷ b</th><th scope="col">Remainder</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1071</td><td>462</td><td>2</td><td>147</td></tr>
          <tr><td>462</td><td>147</td><td>3</td><td>21</td></tr>
          <tr><td>147</td><td>21</td><td>7</td><td>0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The remainder reached zero with a divisor of 21, so GCD(1071, 462) = 21. Any number that divides both
      a and b also divides their remainder, so the pair can shrink without a change to the answer. The
      number of steps grows only logarithmically, so hundred-digit inputs take no noticeable time.
    </p>

    <h2>The prime factorisation method</h2>
    <p>
      Factor each number into primes, then:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>For the GCD, take the lowest power of each prime that appears in every number.</li>
      <li>For the LCM, take the highest power of each prime that appears in any number.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For 48 = 2⁴ × 3 and 180 = 2² × 3² × 5: the GCD takes 2² × 3 = 12, and the LCM takes 2⁴ × 3² × 5 = 720.
      This method is slower for large numbers, because factoring is hard, but the answer is easy to see and
      most classrooms teach it. The tool shows both methods.
    </p>

    <h2>The identity that links them</h2>
    <p>
      For any two numbers, GCD(a, b) × LCM(a, b) = a × b. So the LCM is a × b ÷ GCD. The calculator uses
      this, so it does not have to factor anything. The identity holds for pairs only. For three or more
      numbers, fold pairwise: LCM(a, b, c) = LCM(LCM(a, b), c).
    </p>

    <h2>Where you need these</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>To add fractions, use the LCM of the denominators as the least common denominator. This keeps the numbers small.</li>
      <li>To simplify fractions and ratios, divide by the GCD. This reduces them to lowest terms in one step.</li>
      <li>Two buses that leave every 12 and 18 minutes leave together every LCM(12, 18) = 36 minutes.</li>
      <li>The largest square tile that fits a 48 × 180 cm floor exactly has side GCD(48, 180) = 12 cm.</li>
      <li>Two gears with 8 and 12 teeth return to their start alignment after LCM(8, 12) = 24 teeth pass.</li>
      <li>RSA key generation uses the GCD to test that the public exponent is coprime to Euler’s totient.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quick reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Numbers</th><th scope="col">GCD</th><th scope="col">LCM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>12, 18</td><td>6</td><td>36</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8, 9</td><td>1 (coprime)</td><td>72</td></tr>
          <tr><td>48, 180</td><td>12</td><td>720</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15, 25, 35</td><td>5</td><td>525</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4, 6, 8, 12</td><td>2</td><td>24</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1071, 462</td><td>21</td><td>23,562</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Precision and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      GCD and LCM use arbitrary-precision integers, so inputs of any practical length are exact. The tool
      factors a number into primes only when it is small enough for quick trial division. All work runs in
      your browser and nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
